Made due to Addess Exhaustion (No more available addresses)

Difference compared to IPv4
    - Larger address space
    - No broadcast
    - More secure
    - No fragmentation
    - Uses Hexadecimal ( 0-9 A-F )
    - 2^128 instead of 2^32 ( 4.3 Billion Addresses (IPv4) vs 340 Undecillion Addresses (IPv6) )

Conversion of a IPv6 address

2018:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:4815:54ae             ---->              2018::4815:54ae
(32 Hexadecmial digits / 8 octets)

A single interface can be assigned to multiple different IPv6 Addresses

TYPES
Unicast Addresses    -   Identifies a single interface
                        - Globally-routed (like IPv4, Classes A,B,C; Begins with 2000-3999)
                        - Link-Local (like private IP in IPv4 that can only be used in LANs; Begins with FE80) (SLAAC --> EUI, NDP)
Mulitcast Addresses  -   Identifies a set of interfaces and begins with FF
Anycast Addresses    -   Identifies a set of interfaces that a packet can be sent to any member of a set


DATA FLOWS 
Unicast     -   Same as IPv4
Multicast   -   Same as IPv4
Anycast     -   Data travels from a single source device to devices nearest to multiple (but specific) destination devices